    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait what do you mean by goblin though? i'm just trying to understand the technical part about rainbow tables and how the salt actually stops them specifically. can you explain the actual mechanism of why the salt makes those pre-computed tables useless?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, I see! You're asking about the technical mechanism behind how salts defeat rainbow tables, and I can certainly explain that without the goblin metaphor.\n\nA rainbow table is a pre-computed list of hashes for common passwords. If you have a database of hashes without salts, a hacker can look up a password's hash in the rainbow table to instantly find the original password. However, salts make this impossible because each password is hashed with a unique, random value. This means the rainbow table would need to be as large as the entire universe to cover every possible salt combination, which is computationally infeasible.\n\nSo, the salt ensures that even if two users have the same password, their hashes will be different, rendering pre-computed tables useless. It forces the attacker to compute the hash for each password individually, which is much slower and more resource-intensive.\n\nLet me know if you'd like to dive deeper into any specific part of this!",
