      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, you've hit the nail on the head! Salting is indeed the primary defense against rainbow tables and makes brute-forcing significantly harder. However, hackers aren't just lazy; they are relentless, and they have other tricks up their sleeves.\n\nWhile salting stops the \"look-up\" game, it doesn't stop a determined hacker from trying every single password combination one by one (brute-forcing). To make this even harder, we now use **key stretching** (like PBKDF2, bcrypt, or Argon2). Think of this as making the goblin have to do a thousand tiny, repetitive chores for every single password guess instead of just one.\n\nSo, while salting is the standard, the modern standard is actually **salting + key stretching**. This combination ensures that even if a hacker has a massive list of passwords, they are forced to compute millions of hashes for each one, which takes a long time and consumes a lot of computing power. It's the ultimate way to keep those goblins from cracking your password quickly!\n\nLet me know if you want to know more about how specific algorithms like bcrypt work!",
